11:11:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Good question — but the premise has a common misconception in it: water doesn't need to reach 100°C to evaporate. Here's what's actually going on. **Boiling vs. evaporation are different things** - **Boiling** happens at 100°C (at sea level pressure), where water turns to vapor rapidly throughout the whole liquid, forming bubbles. - **Evaporation** happens at *any* temperature, even well below freezing, but more slowly and only at the surface of the liquid. **Why evaporation happens below 100°C** Water molecules are in constant random motion, and their energy varies — some move fast, some slow (this is just statistics, like a bell curve of speeds). Even at room temperature or in cold water, some molecules at the surface have *enough* energy to break free from the liquid and become vapor, even though the *average* energy of all the molecules is way below what's needed to boil. The sun speeds this up by: 1. **Adding energy** – Sunlight (mostly infrared and some visible light) is absorbed by water, heating it. Higher temperature means more molecules have enough energy to escape as vapor. 2. **Shifting the whole distribution** – warming the water shifts more molecules into that "fast enough to escape" range, so evaporation happens faster with more sun. **A puddle example** You've probably seen a puddle disappear on a sunny day even though it never got close to boiling. That's ordinary evaporation, accelerated by solar heating — not boiling. So the sun doesn't need to boil water to evaporate it — it just needs to add enough energy to help surface molecules escape, and warmer temperatures (from sunlight) make that happen much faster.
